Found while measuring karr #123 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops). Pre-existing, not
introduced or moved by that change -- filed rather than acted on.

ADR 0013s foreign-resolution guard refuses a leaf whose emitted token Go
resolves differently from what the MAC digest covers. For a leaf this library
types `int` or `float` that is right and sops agrees: `mode: 0755` is 493 to
sops, and ADR 0013 recorded the maintainer decision not to retype it by
quoting.

But the same guard also fires for a leaf that is ALREADY a STRING, where
quoting retypes nothing and is exactly what sops does. Measured, one document
per row, leaf under _unencrypted, source value quoted in the plaintext so both
implementations agree it is a string:

  source          sops -e wire        sops -d   File::SOPS->encrypt_file
  ".inf"          "\".inf\""          exit 0    CROAK
  "1_000"         "\"1_000\""         exit 0    CROAK
  "2015-01-01"    "\"2015-01-01\""    exit 0    CROAK
  "0o10"          "\"0o10\""          exit 0    CROAK
  "0755"          "\"0755\""          exit 0    written as '0755'  (YAML::XS quotes
                                                this one by itself)

So four of five are documents sops writes, sops reads, and this library
refuses to produce. The fifth is the shape of the fix: YAML::XSs own
$YAML::XS::QuoteNumericStrings already quotes a string its resolver would read
as a number, and the gap is only the spellings libyamls resolver does not
recognise but go-yamls does.

WHY THIS IS NOT THE ALTERNATIVE ADR 0013 REJECTED. There the objection was
that quoting CHANGES THE TYPE -- `mode: 0755` would become a string where the
callers parser said integer, and sops would have written the integer 493.
Here the leaf is a string on both sides already; quoting it states the type the
document has rather than inventing one, and sops itself writes exactly those
bytes. The two cases are separable by what detect_type says about the leaf, and
the guard currently does not separate them.

NOT to be confused with a BARE .inf in a plaintext (karr #123): there the leaf
is a float to sops, and the answer is to resolve it, not to quote it.

Format lane (Format::YAML::_reject_foreign_resolution and the emitter), with a
corpus per row and its own ADR -- it changes bytes on the wire, so it is a
wire-lane handover if the emitted document moves for any leaf that is written
today.
CONFIRMED A REAL DEFECT AND HANDED TO THE WIRE LANE -- docs/adr/0038 (measured jointly with #127, #128, #131: 50 scalars x 3 slot configurations, sops 3.13.3, each sops_mac decrypted and the digest SOLVED per row).

WIDER THAN FILED. Not 4 rows, 22. In the unencrypted slot the guard refuses 28 of 50
scalars; 6 of those refusals are right (#127's 0755/010/!!int 0755 and #128's three
timestamp spellings -- sops writes the RESOLVED form and never the source spelling).
The other 22 are strings sops writes double-quoted and reads back at exit 0:
  ".inf" ".Inf" ".INF" "+.inf" "-.inf" ".nan" ".NaN"
  "1_000" "0_7" "685_230.15"
  "2015-01-01" "2015-1-2" "2015-01-01t12:00:00Z" "2015-01-01 12:00:00"
  "0o10" "0O10" "0x1f" "0b101"
  "Null" "NULL" "TRUE" "FALSE"
The 22 written rows in the same slot are byte-identical to sops's output, all exit 0.
This is the ONLY one of the four tickets in the class 'sops writes it, sops reads it,
we refuse' -- the class fixed in karr #102, #105, #116, #118.

WHY THE FIFTH ROW IS DIFFERENT. Probed over 68 spellings: ::XS::QuoteNumericStrings
quotes a string that looks like a DECIMAL number ('0755' '007' '08' '1e3' '-010'), plus
lower-case 'true' 'false' 'null' '~' and the empty string, and NOTHING else. It leaves
.inf .nan Null NULL TRUE FALSE True False yes on 1:30 0x1f 1_000 and every date bare --
even the ones libyaml's own RESOLVER reads as YAML 1.1 types. So _emitted_plain_scalar
returns undef for "0755" and the guard returns before any verdict. The whole difference
is the width of one emitter option.

THE MESSAGE IS WRONG TOO. For a string leaf the croak says 'sops itself resolves such a
spelling when it writes: a plaintext mode: 0755 becomes the integer 493'. True for an int
leaf, false for these -- sops resolves nothing, it quotes the string and keeps it.

HANDOVER, and the three reasons are measured rather than read off the lane boundary:
  (a) It moves bytes on the wire: 22 leaves that produce no document today would produce
      one. That is this ticket's own handover condition.
  (b) YAML::XS has NO per-scalar style control -- no tag, no forced-quote hook, as
      _quote_sops_timestamp has recorded since 0.003. The only route is rewriting the
      text after Dump, and doing that for arbitrary DATA leaves rather than one metadata
      key in one block is a new mechanism on the wire path.
  (c) THE OBVIOUS FIX IS WRONG. 'Quote every str leaf the guard would refuse' also
      silently changes #128's unencrypted half: bare 'v_unencrypted: 2015-01-01' and
      quoted 'v_unencrypted: "2015-01-01"' arrive as the SAME Perl string (verified,
      both [2015-01-01] length 10). The bare one is a time to sops, normalised to
      2015-01-01T00:00:00Z; quoting it turns today's loud refusal into a silent
      divergence -- the direction ADR 0032 refused for !!bool True.

WHERE THE FIX LIVES. The plain/quoted distinction is in the SOURCE TEXT, and this module
already makes it for one token family: _restore_plain_infinities re-parses with a
plain-style loader so a bare .inf becomes a float (ADR 0026) while a quoted ".inf" stays
a string (ADR 0034). Measured, that asymmetry is visible from outside TODAY:
    v_unencrypted: .inf     -> WRITTEN as .inf
    v_unencrypted: ".inf"   -> CROAK
    v_unencrypted: .nan     -> WRITTEN as .nan
    v_unencrypted: ".nan"   -> CROAK
That is both the proof a correct fix is reachable with machinery this module already has,
and the proof it needs plain/quoted state carried from parse into emit. New cross-phase
state on the wire path is not the format lane's to add.

ENCRYPTED SLOT IS CLEAN: all 36 string rows written here are byte-identical to sops's,
36/36 sops -d exit 0. JSON is clean too: 10 spellings, both slots, 10/10 identical label,
plaintext and slot text. The defect is YAML + unencrypted slot only.
WIRE LANE, MEASURED AND DECIDED -- docs/adr/0039. The refusal STAYS; the message
is corrected; nothing on the wire moves.

WHY NOT FIXED, in the order the handover asked:
 (1) THE CARRIER ROUTE (ADR 0037) IS DEAD, and dead in the worse direction.
     6 SV shapes x 9 spellings through Dump: no shape makes YAML::XS quote
     anything it would not quote anyway, because its rule reads the SV --
     dualvar(0,'0755') writes 0755 BARE, i.e. a carrier REMOVES the one quoting
     we get today. ADR 0037's carrier works there because its target form is a
     PLAIN token; here the target form is the one thing a carrier cannot make.
 (2) NO WIDER EMITTER OPTION. $YAML::XS::QuoteNumericStrings probed at 0/1/2/3
     over 19 spellings: it is a BOOLEAN (1, 2, 3 byte-identical) and quotes
     exactly what Perl's looks_like_number accepts, plus lower-case
     true/false/null/~ and the empty string. No other knob exists in the module.
 (3) NO IN-WALK REPLACEMENT. reject_scalar's return value is discarded by
     Encrypted::_written_leaf by construction, so surgery would need a SECOND
     full tree walk inside emit (or an edit to canonical_float_tree -- another
     lane's file, with karr #122 in flight).
 (4) THE AMBIGUITY IS 15 OF 22, not 4. Measured, bare vs quoted source through
     Format::YAML->parse, compared by detect_type + value_to_bytes: 15 arrive as
     the IDENTICAL Perl string; only the 7 non-finite tokens differ, and only
     because ADR 0026/0034 already resolve a plain one at parse the way Go does.
     The date family is 4 of those 15. Measured what sops writes from a BARE
     source (44 sops -e runs, all exit 0): 1_000 -> 1000, 0x1f -> 31,
     Null -> null, TRUE -> true, 2015-01-01 -> 2015-01-01T00:00:00Z. Quoting
     would overwrite that silently -- ADR 0032's direction.
 (5) SENTINEL TEXT SURGERY was costed and rejected: it buys 7 of 23 rows -- the
     rarest family -- for a new write-path mechanism, and pre-empts karr #99 in
     passing, which is the one thing ADR 0019 asked the next lane not to do.

WHAT UNBLOCKS THE TICKET, with numbers: karr #99 (an emitter that can quote one
scalar) alone makes 7 of 22 writable; karr #99 + karr #127 (a plain scalar
resolved as Go resolves it, at parse) makes all 22 writable, because the
bare/quoted ambiguity disappears where the information still exists.

A 23RD ROW, same class, found here: "0xffffffffffffffff" -- sops writes it
double-quoted and reads it back at exit 0, we refuse it (the guard's model
cannot decide a uint64, ADR 0013). Its neighbour "9223372036854775808" IS
written, because looks_like_number is true and YAML::XS quotes it for us.

ALSO MEASURED: the READ direction has never been the defect -- 22 of 22
sops-written documents read back here with the MAC verified, in both slots. And
both remedies the message recommends work: encrypt the leaf, 22/22 sops -d exit
0; JSON, both slots, 22/22.

